<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Presbyterian Church of Wales</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Calvinistic Methodist Church</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Presbyterian Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, long known as the Calvinistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methodism">Methodist Church</a>, grew out of the Welsh Methodist revival of the 18th century led by Howell Harris and Daniel Rowland, and became a separate denomination in 1811. It shaped Welsh-language religious life and from 1840 sent missionaries to the Khasi and Jaintia Hills of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the church it founded became one of the largest in the region.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The movement's roots are traced to Griffith Jones, rector of Llanddowror, whose charity schools and preaching drew wide audiences; Daniel Rowland, curate of Llangeitho, was moved by his preaching. Howell Harris underwent a conversion in 1735 and became an itinerant preacher who set up societies; his convert William Williams of Pantycelyn became the hymn-writer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. In January 1743 George Whitefield chaired a meeting at Plas Watford near Caerphilly that organised the societies, the first Methodist Association. A doctrinal quarrel between Rowland and Harris split the societies in 1751 until the revival of 1762 repaired the breach, and Llangeitho became the centre of Welsh Methodism.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In North Wales Thomas Charles of Bala led the movement from 1784 with his charity and Sunday schools. Fines forced the meeting-houses to be registered as dissenting chapels, and in 1811 the lay members, with Charles, carried the ordination of nine ministers at Bala in June and thirteen at Llandilo in August. A Confession of Faith of 44 articles, based on the Westminster Confession, followed in 1823.</p><p>Colleges opened at Bala in 1837, Trevecca in 1842 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberystwyth">Aberystwyth</a> in 1906. Missionary collections went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> until a society of the church's own was formed in 1840; its work in the Khasi and Jaintia Hills, the Lushai Hills and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a> produced the Presbyterian Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>The revival of 1904-05 brought membership to its highest level, approaching 350,000, around 17 to 20 per cent of the Welsh population. In 1928 the church adopted the name Presbyterian Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, and in 1978 Pamela Turner became the first woman ordained as a minister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Known in Welsh as the Hen Gorff, the Old Body, the church mixes Presbyterian and Congregational order: each church manages its own affairs and reports to district and monthly meetings, now called presbyteries, which send delegates to the Associations of the North, the South and the East, the last for English-speaking churches. A general assembly established in 1864 meets once a year. Chapels are named after places in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. In June 2022 it recorded about 12,938 members in around 471 churches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Presbyterian Church of Wales” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British founded a station at Cherrapunji in the 1830s, and from 1841 <b>Welsh Calvinistic Methodist</b> missionaries led by Thomas Jones worked there, writing Khasi in the Latin alphabet.</blockquote><a href="/state/sohra-state">Sohra State</a></li></ul></section>
